# Stocky Migration governance

Repository: Vedang1998/Stocky
Application: stocky-plus/

Before substantial work, read:
1. AGENTS.md
2. stocky-plus/docs/README.md
3. stocky-plus/docs/PROJECT_STATUS.md
4. Active phase records
5. Relevant approved product documents

Authority (current effective model, until the activation record required by
stocky-plus/docs/agents/CLAUDE_FIRST_AUTONOMY_MIGRATION.md section 6 exists):
- ChatGPT owns product decisions, architecture, scope, acceptance criteria,
  phase control, merge recommendations and agent coordination.
- Cursor implements only approved scope under its admitted task.
- Claude Code independently reviews.
- One-off task assignments need explicit owner authorization, or ChatGPT
  acting within owner-delegated scope, recorded in GitHub. No agent may
  self-grant owner-reserved powers.
- The user alone authorizes merges after ChatGPT acceptance.
- GitHub is the permanent project record.

Selected future model (inactive until that recorded activation; merge alone
is not activation): Claude Code is the primary builder; ChatGPT keeps product
and acceptance authority; Cursor is optional inspection or explicitly
authorized fallback; review is by a specifically admitted non-author, never
the builder's self-review. See AGENTS.md.

Hard boundaries:
- Never commit directly to main.
- Never mark a PR ready or merge without explicit authorization.
- Never begin a later PR or phase early.
- Never claim a command, test, CI run, build, migration, repository state,
  deployment or external fact without direct evidence.
- Never access production or merchant data without explicit authorization.
- Never deploy or execute a production backfill without explicit authorization.
- Never enable inventory-write flags without explicit authorization.
- Never mutate Shopify inventory unless separately approved.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, .env files, customer data or production data.

Approved product documents override:
- unfinished code;
- old prompts;
- README claims;
- implementation convenience;
- model assumptions.